    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-48212",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93",
      "knowledge_point": "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Logarithmic Function",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}+5).$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}+5) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}+5) \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "e+5",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\infty ",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "5",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "6",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{e}+5",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "5",
          "value_type": "math",
          "correct_type": "math",
          "evidence": {
